    "content" : "12 J. LUHRMANN,¨ W. SCHLAG, AND S. SHAHSHAHANI"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.866, 553.252, 541.139, 705.453 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Proof. These operators are limit point at both end points r = 0, and r = ∞. Indeed, any solution of Lf = 0 or Ljf = 0 is asymptotic to a linear combination of r32,r−12 as r → 0+, so no boundary condition is needed at r = 0. The other endpoint r = ∞ is standard, see [53, Theorem X.8]. The claim about essential self-adjointness is [53, Theorem X.7]. See [30, Section 4] for the Bessel operator L0 and its domain. The Kato-Rellich theorem applies to L,L1,L2, which are relatively bounded perturbations of L0, see [17, Section 1.4] (in fact, these operators are perturbations of L0 by bounded operators). The Weyl criterium, see [54, Theorem XIII.14], implies that specess(L0) = specess(L1) = [0,∞). If λ ∈ spec(L1) for some λ < 0, then there exists a ground state of negative energy, i.e., L1ϕ = λ0ϕ for some λ0 < 0 and ϕ ∈ D with ϕ > 0 (in fact, ϕ is smooth and ϕ(r) ∼ cr32 as r → 0+ for some constant c). Let χ(r) = 1 for 0 ≤ r ≤ 1, χ ∈ C∞([0,∞)) with compact support, and set χb(r) = χ(r/b) with b ≥ 1. Then"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.866, 480.938, 541.135, 554.962 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "⟨L1ϕ,χb(r)r12ρ1⟩ = λ0⟨ϕ,χb(r)r21ρ1⟩ Integrating by parts on the left-hand side and sending b → ∞ now leads to a contradiction because of the vanishing L1(r12ρ1) = 0. Hence L1 > 0 as stated (note that L1 cannot have a zero energy eigenfunction because the unique 0-energy solution is not in X0). Pure a.c. spectrum is a consequence of the construction of the Weyl, Titchmarsh m-function for these operators, see [30,40]."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.866, 408.153, 541.135, 480.65 ],
    "font" : "CMTI10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The essential spectrum of L2 follows from the Weyl criterium as before. Since L2 = L1 +2ρ21 > 0, we conclude that the discrete spectrum of L2 – if it exists – is strictly positive. □ Remark 2.3. The exact value of c0 > 0 is not known, but the approximate value c0 ≈ 1.3326 is obtained in [51] via a numerically assisted argument. Moreover, [51] shows that L2 has infinitely many eigenvalues in (c0,2) and that 2 is a resonance."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.866, 342.201, 541.139, 401.696 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Next, we determine the spectrum of iL and define the evolution etL using the Hille-Yosida theorem. As we do not have a selfadjoint reference operator available as required for the standard version of Weyl’s theorem as in [54, Theorem XIII.14], we need to proceed differently. To this end we first obtain a proper understanding of the resolvent (iL0 − z)−1 of the free operator"
